#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Written one-line takeaways for Luke.

Each states what the verse turns on. Narrative verses that carry no
standalone point are left out deliberately; the drawer hides the card
rather than showing filler.
"""

TAKEAWAYS = {
    # ---- Chapter 1 ----
    '1:1': "Luke begins by admitting others wrote first. The gospel reaches us as reported fact, not private revelation.",
    '1:2': "\"Eyewitnesses, and ministers of the word\" — the same men who saw it are the men who preached it.",
    '1:3': "\"In order\" is not piety about method. Luke means the account to be checked.",
    '1:4': "\"That thou mightest know the certainty.\" The gospel is written to settle faith, not to stir feeling.",
    '1:5': "Luke dates the story by Herod's reign. Salvation happens inside somebody's calendar.",
    '1:6': "Both righteous, both blameless, and still childless. Obedience buys no exemption from disappointment.",
    '1:7': "\"Because that Elisabeth was barren.\" The reason is stated flatly, with no blame attached to it.",
    '1:9': "\"His lot was to burn incense.\" The once-in-a-lifetime assignment fell to him by lot, not by merit.",
    '1:10': "The people are praying outside at the very hour the answer is being spoken within.",
    '1:12': "Fear, not delight, is the first reaction to an angel. Scripture is consistent about this.",
    '1:13': "\"Thy prayer is heard\" — years after he had stopped expecting it, and likely stopped praying it.",
    '1:15': "John is filled with the Spirit before he has done anything at all.",
    '1:16': "John's work is measured in people turned back, not in crowds gathered.",
    '1:17': "\"The hearts of the fathers to the children.\" Preparing a people for the Lord starts in households.",
    '1:18': "\"Whereby shall I know this?\" Zacharias asks for proof; Mary will only ask how. The difference is unbelief.",
    '1:19': "Gabriel answers the doubt by naming who sent him. A promise is worth what its maker is worth.",
    '1:20': "The silence is discipline, not cancellation. The word will be fulfilled in its season either way.",
    '1:21': "The congregation waits outside for a blessing that is not coming; the priest has been struck dumb.",
    '1:22': "He can no longer pronounce the benediction, which is the one thing the office was for.",
    '1:24': "Elisabeth hides five months. Luke leaves her reason unexplained rather than inventing one.",
    '1:25': "\"To take away my reproach among men.\" Childlessness was a public shame, and she names it as such.",
    '1:26': "Gabriel is sent from the temple in Jerusalem to Nazareth — from the centre to a town of no account.",
    '1:28': "\"Highly favoured\" is something done to her. It describes God's disposition, not her qualifications.",
    '1:29': "She is troubled at the saying, not at the angel. The praise is what unsettles her.",
    '1:30': "\"Thou hast found favour with God.\" Favour found, not earned; the word underneath it is grace.",
    '1:31': "Mary is given the name before the child exists. The mission is fixed before the biography.",
    '1:32': "The throne of David is given to him. A kingdom conferred, not a kingdom seized.",
    '1:33': "\"Of his kingdom there shall be no end.\" Every other reign named in Luke's opening has an end date.",
    '1:34': "\"How shall this be?\" Not a demand for evidence but a question about the mechanics.",
    '1:35': "The Spirit overshadows; the child is called holy from conception, not made holy afterward.",
    '1:36': "Mary is handed a sign she never asked for: a relative's pregnancy she can go and see for herself.",
    '1:37': "\"With God nothing shall be impossible\" is said about a womb, not about ambition in general.",
    '1:38': "\"Be it unto me according to thy word.\" She receives the promise; she does not negotiate its terms.",
    '1:41': "The unborn child reacts before either woman has explained anything. The Spirit moves first.",
    '1:42': "Elisabeth, who has a miracle of her own, blesses the younger woman without a trace of rivalry.",
    '1:43': "\"The mother of my Lord.\" She calls him Lord months before he is born.",
    '1:44': "Her evidence is what her own body did, not a conclusion she reasoned her way to.",
    '1:45': "Mary is called blessed for believing, not for bearing. Faith is what Elisabeth singles out.",
    '1:46': "\"My soul doth magnify the Lord.\" The song enlarges the view of God, not the view of Mary.",
    '1:47': "\"God my Saviour.\" The mother of Christ counts herself among those who need saving.",
    '1:48': "\"Low estate\" is the stated reason. God's regard falls on the unimportant by preference.",
    '1:50': "Mercy is described as generational, outlasting any single person's experience of it.",
    '1:51': "The proud are scattered \"in the imagination of their hearts.\" Judgment reaches the inner room.",
    '1:52': "Thrones emptied and the low seated. Luke's whole gospel is contained in that exchange.",
    '1:53': "\"The rich he hath sent empty away.\" The full leave with nothing because they arrived lacking nothing.",
    '1:55': "The song ends in Abraham. What is happening to Mary is an old promise keeping its word.",
    '1:59': "They move to name him after his father. Custom assumes a child belongs to what came before.",
    '1:60': "\"Not so; but he shall be called John.\" The mother overrules the custom on a word she was not given.",
    '1:62': "They make signs to a man who was struck dumb, not deaf. Assumptions about the afflicted come fast.",
    '1:63': "\"His name is John.\" The first thing Zacharias does with his hand is agree with God.",
    '1:64': "Speech returns at the moment of obedience, not at the moment the promise was first given.",
    '1:65': "Fear spreads through the hill country. God acting nearby is not a comfortable thing.",
    '1:66': "\"What manner of child shall this be?\" The neighbours ask it, and the book takes chapters to answer.",
    '1:68': "The song opens with God having visited and redeemed — past tense, before anything visible has happened.",
    '1:69': "\"An horn of salvation\" is strength, not sentiment, and it is raised up in David's house.",
    '1:71': "Zacharias expects rescue from enemies. Deliverance comes, but not in the shape he is picturing.",
    '1:72': "Mercy is called promised and remembered: God holding himself to what he said in advance.",
    '1:73': "The oath sworn to Abraham is the security. The covenant rests on God's swearing, not ours.",
    '1:74': "Delivered in order to serve without fear. Freedom here is freedom for service, not from it.",
    '1:76': "A father's prophecy over his newborn son is almost entirely about somebody else's arrival.",
    '1:77': "Salvation is defined here as the remission of sins, not as the rescue of a nation.",
    '1:78': "\"The tender mercy of our God\" is given as the cause of the whole thing. Nothing here was owed.",
    '1:79': "The light comes to those sitting in darkness. They were not walking toward it.",
    '1:80': "John grows up in the deserts, unseen for decades before the day of his shewing unto Israel.",
}

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 2 ----
    '2:1': "An imperial tax decree moves a pregnant woman across the country. Caesar serves a purpose he never hears of.",
    '2:2': "Luke pins the census to a named governor of Syria. He wants the date checkable.",
    '2:4': "Joseph goes to Bethlehem because of his lineage. The bureaucracy delivers the prophecy's address.",
    '2:7': "\"No room for them in the inn.\" The world's first act toward him is failing to make space.",
    '2:8': "Shepherds are working the night watch when the announcement comes. The news finds men at their jobs.",
    '2:9': "Glory and terror arrive together. \"Sore afraid\" is the honest response to God at close range.",
    '2:10': "\"To all people\" — the angel widens the audience before anyone can ask whether it includes them.",
    '2:11': "\"Unto you is born.\" The possessive comes before the titles Saviour, Christ and Lord.",
    '2:12': "The sign is an infant in a feed trough. Nothing about it looks like evidence.",
    '2:14': "Glory to God and peace on earth are one event described twice, not two separate wishes.",
    '2:15': "The shepherds treat an angelic announcement as something to go and verify.",
    '2:16': "They find exactly what they were told and nothing more impressive. The sign held.",
    '2:17': "Shepherds become the first preachers. The witnesses God picked would not have been believed in court.",
    '2:19': "Mary keeps and ponders rather than speaks. Much of this chapter probably came from her.",
    '2:20': "They return to the same flock, praising. The encounter did not relieve them of their work.",
    '2:21': "Circumcised and named on the eighth day: placed under the law before he could consent to it.",
    '2:22': "The rite of purification is kept for a child who had nothing to be purified from.",
    '2:24': "Two pigeons was the offering allowed to those who could not afford a lamb. The family is poor.",
    '2:25': "Simeon is \"waiting for the consolation of Israel\" — a whole life arranged around an unkept promise.",
    '2:26': "He is promised he will not die before seeing Christ, which is also a promise that he will die after.",
    '2:27': "He came by the Spirit, and the child arrived. Luke declines to call the timing coincidence.",
    '2:28': "He takes God's salvation up in his arms. It has weight; it can be held.",
    '2:29': "\"Now lettest thou thy servant depart in peace.\" Having seen Christ, he asks for dismissal, not more time.",
    '2:30': "\"Mine eyes have seen thy salvation.\" Salvation is a person who can be looked at, not a state of feeling.",
    '2:31': "Prepared \"before the face of all people\" — public from the start, not a private consolation.",
    '2:32': "Light for the Gentiles and glory for Israel, both carried by the same infant.",
    '2:33': "Joseph and his mother marvelled. Even those told the most are still learning what they were told.",
    '2:34': "\"Set for the fall and rising again of many.\" He divides before he gathers; no one stays neutral.",
    '2:35': "The sword through Mary's soul is spoken inside a blessing. Nothing here is promised to be painless.",
    '2:36': "Anna is given a name, a father and a tribe. Luke credentials a woman as carefully as any man.",
    '2:37': "Fourscore and four years, most of them widowed, and the temple is where she kept turning up.",
    '2:38': "She speaks of him to all who looked for redemption. Jerusalem's first evangelist is an old widow.",
    '2:40': "\"The child grew.\" Whatever else is true of him, he did not arrive finished.",
    '2:43': "The parents leave without him and do not know it. Losing Christ can happen unnoticed.",
    '2:44': "They searched among kinsfolk and acquaintance, which is exactly where he was not.",
    '2:46': "Three days missing, and he is in the temple, listening and asking questions.",
    '2:47': "The astonishment is at his understanding and his answers, not at any wonder performed.",
    '2:48': "\"Thy father and I have sought thee sorrowing.\" Mary says father, and the next verse corrects her.",
    '2:49': "\"My Father's business.\" His first recorded words place his allegiance above his household.",
    '2:50': "\"They understood not.\" Proximity to Jesus did not spare his own family confusion.",
    '2:51': "\"Was subject unto them.\" The boy about his Father's business goes home and obeys his parents.",
    '2:52': "Increase in wisdom, stature and favour: a real human life lived forward, not a performance of one.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 3 ----
    '3:1': "Six rulers are named to date one sentence. Luke plants the gospel in documented history.",
    '3:2': "The word of God bypasses Annas and Caiaphas and lands on a man in the wilderness.",
    '3:3': "Baptism of repentance \"for the remission of sins\" — the washing points at forgiveness, not at resolve.",
    '3:4': "The voice belongs to the wilderness. The way is prepared where nobody is watching.",
    '3:5': "Valleys filled and mountains levelled. Preparing his way means the ground stops having ranks.",
    '3:6': "\"All flesh shall see the salvation of God.\" Luke quotes Isaiah further than the others do, out to everyone.",
    '3:7': "He greets the crowd that came to be baptized as a generation of vipers. No welcome, no easing in.",
    '3:8': "\"We have Abraham to our father\" is the exact plea John forbids. Inherited standing counts for nothing.",
    '3:9': "The axe is already at the root. John's preaching leaves no time to improve at leisure.",
    '3:10': "\"What shall we do then?\" Law preached honestly produces this question, not enthusiasm.",
    '3:11': "The answer is a coat and a meal. Repentance lands in the cupboard before it reaches the temple.",
    '3:12': "Publicans come to be baptized. The trade everyone despised is not told to find another trade.",
    '3:13': "\"Exact no more than that which is appointed you.\" The tax collector is sent back to tax honestly.",
    '3:14': "Soldiers are told to stop extorting and be content, not to leave the army. Vocation is reformed, not abandoned.",
    '3:15': "The crowd is ready to make John the Christ. Popularity offers him a title he never sought.",
    '3:16': "John measures himself by a shoe latchet he is not fit to untie, and hands the crowd on.",
    '3:17': "The winnowing fan is in Christ's hand, not John's. The sorting belongs to the one who comes after.",
    '3:19': "John reproves a tetrarch about his brother's wife. The preaching did not stop at safe targets.",
    '3:20': "Herod \"added yet this above all\": silencing the reproof is counted as the worst of his evils.",
    '3:21': "Jesus is baptized among all the people, and he is praying when heaven opens. Luke keeps the prayer in.",
    '3:22': "\"Thou art my beloved Son\" is said before a single miracle. The approval precedes the ministry.",
    '3:23': "\"As was supposed\" — Luke flags the legal fiction in the middle of the family tree.",
    '3:31': "The line runs through Nathan rather than Solomon, away from the royal succession everyone would expect.",
    '3:32': "Jesse, Obed and Boaz: the line passes through a Moabite marriage in a Bethlehem barley field.",
    '3:38': "\"Adam, which was the son of God.\" Luke traces him to the whole race, not to Israel alone.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 4 ----
    '4:1': "Full of the Spirit, and led by the Spirit into the wilderness. The Spirit leads into the testing, not around it.",
    '4:2': "Forty days of temptation, and the hunger comes at the end. The hardest hour is the last one.",
    '4:3': "\"If thou be the Son of God\" — the attack goes straight at the voice that spoke at the Jordan.",
    '4:4': "He answers hunger with scripture rather than with power he plainly had.",
    '4:5': "All the kingdoms of the world in a moment. The offer is real enough to be worth refusing.",
    '4:6': "The devil claims the kingdoms are his to hand over. The lie is plausible because it is partly true.",
    '4:7': "One act of worship for everything. The price is always lower than the thing is worth.",
    '4:8': "\"Him only shalt thou serve.\" Worship of God and possession of the world are set as alternatives.",
    '4:9': "The last temptation is staged at the temple. The holiest address is not the safest one.",
    '4:10': "The devil quotes scripture accurately and uses it to push Jesus off a roof.",
    '4:11': "A promise of protection is turned into a dare. Presumption borrows the language of trust.",
    '4:12': "\"Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God.\" Testing God is the opposite of trusting him, not proof of it.",
    '4:13': "\"For a season.\" The devil leaves on a timetable, and Luke means us to remember it at Gethsemane.",
    '4:16': "\"As his custom was, he went into the synagogue.\" The Son of God kept regular attendance.",
    '4:17': "He is handed the scroll and finds the place. The reading is chosen, not stumbled upon.",
    '4:18': "Poor, brokenhearted, captive, blind, bruised. He names his congregation before he names his message.",
    '4:19': "\"The acceptable year of the Lord\" — jubilee language, debts cancelled rather than rescheduled.",
    '4:20': "He closes the book and sits down, and every eye follows him. Luke holds the silence.",
    '4:21': "\"This day is this scripture fulfilled in your ears.\" The prophecy is not explained; it is claimed.",
    '4:22': "They admire the gracious words and then reduce him to Joseph's son. Familiarity does the damage.",
    '4:23': "\"Physician, heal thyself.\" The hometown wants the miracles it heard about, as its due.",
    '4:24': "No prophet is accepted in his own country. Nearness to Jesus is not the same as receiving him.",
    '4:25': "Many widows in Israel, and the famine spared nobody. The point is coming and they can feel it.",
    '4:26': "Elijah was sent to a Sidonian widow, past every hungry Israelite. God's mercy chose an outsider.",
    '4:27': "\"None of them was cleansed, saving Naaman the Syrian.\" Grace given to an enemy officer.",
    '4:28': "The sermon that emptied the room was about God's kindness to foreigners.",
    '4:29': "The congregation that admired him an hour ago is marching him to a cliff.",
    '4:30': "He passes through the middle of them. The hour is not theirs to set.",
    '4:32': "\"His word was with power.\" The astonishment is at the authority, before any healing is done.",
    '4:34': "The demon has the doctrine exactly right: \"the Holy One of God.\" Correct theology is not faith.",
    '4:35': "\"Hold thy peace, and come out.\" Jesus refuses the demon's testimony even though it is accurate.",
    '4:36': "They marvel at the word rather than the gesture. Authority is the thing they cannot place.",
    '4:38': "The first thing he does after the synagogue is walk into a house where somebody is ill.",
    '4:39': "He rebukes the fever as he rebuked the demon, and she gets up and serves. Restored to her work, not to bed.",
    '4:40': "\"He laid his hands on every one of them.\" A whole town's sick, and he treats them one at a time.",
    '4:41': "The demons confess him as Christ and are silenced. He will not have his identity announced by them.",
    '4:42': "He goes to a desert place at daybreak; the crowd finds him and tries to keep him.",
    '4:43': "\"Therefore am I sent.\" Being wanted in one town does not settle where he is meant to be.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 5 ----
    '5:3': "He borrows a working boat for a pulpit. Simon's tools are put to a use Simon had not imagined.",
    '5:4': "A carpenter tells fishermen where the fish are, in daylight, in deep water.",
    '5:5': "\"Nevertheless at thy word.\" Simon obeys against his own expertise, and says so plainly.",
    '5:6': "The nets break. The answer is more than the equipment was built to hold.",
    '5:7': "The catch is too big for one crew. Abundance forces him to call in the partners.",
    '5:8': "\"Depart from me; for I am a sinful man.\" Kindness, not judgment, is what exposes Peter to himself.",
    '5:10': "\"Fear not; from henceforth thou shalt catch men.\" The confession of sin is answered with a commission.",
    '5:11': "They leave the largest catch of their lives lying on the shore.",
    '5:12': "A man full of leprosy asks only whether Jesus is willing. He never doubts that he is able.",
    '5:13': "He touched him. The untouchable man is healed by the one thing nobody had done for him in years.",
    '5:14': "He is sent to the priest with Moses' offering. The healed man is put back inside the community's law.",
    '5:16': "\"He withdrew himself into the wilderness, and prayed.\" Luke records the retreat at the height of the demand.",
    '5:17': "The experts have come from every town to sit and watch, and the power of the Lord was present to heal.",
    '5:19': "Friends who cannot get through the door take the roof off. Faith here is stubborn and inconvenient.",
    '5:20': "\"When he saw their faith\" — the faith Jesus responds to belongs to the men carrying the bed.",
    '5:21': "Their objection is sound theology: only God forgives sins. They have reasoned their way right up to him.",
    '5:23': "Forgiveness is the easier thing to say precisely because nobody can check it.",
    '5:24': "The healing is offered as proof of the forgiving, not the other way round.",
    '5:25': "He carries home the bed that had carried him.",
    '5:26': "Amazement, praise and fear together. \"Strange things\" is an honest account of what they saw.",
    '5:27': "Jesus sees a tax collector at his post and says Follow me. No interview, no probation.",
    '5:28': "\"He left all, rose up, and followed him.\" The booth is abandoned in the middle of a working day.",
    '5:29': "Levi's first act as a disciple is to throw a party and invite everyone he knows.",
    '5:30': "The complaint is about the table. Who Jesus eats with offends more than what he teaches.",
    '5:31': "\"They that are whole need not a physician.\" The objection is answered by naming what a doctor is for.",
    '5:32': "\"I came not to call the righteous.\" The self-satisfied are not excluded; they have excused themselves.",
    '5:33': "The complaint shifts to fasting. When the table fails to scandalise, the calendar will do.",
    '5:34': "Fasting at a wedding would misrepresent the occasion. His presence sets what is fitting.",
    '5:35': "\"The bridegroom shall be taken away.\" He knows already how this ends, and says so at a feast.",
    '5:36': "A patch off a new coat ruins both garments. The gospel is not a repair to the old arrangement.",
    '5:37': "New wine bursts old skins. What he brings cannot be stored in the containers built for the old way.",
    '5:38': "\"Both are preserved\" — the point is not destruction of the old but fitness to hold what is given.",
    '5:39': "\"The old is better.\" Luke alone records this line, and it explains most resistance to grace.",

    # ---- Chapter 6 ----
    '6:1': "Hungry men rubbing grain in their hands on a sabbath. The controversy starts in a cornfield, over a snack.",
    '6:2': "The Pharisees address the disciples, not Jesus. The rule is enforced sideways.",
    '6:4': "David ate the shewbread reserved for priests, and scripture does not condemn him. Need outranked the rubric.",
    '6:5': "\"Lord also of the sabbath.\" The day has an owner, and the owner is standing in the field.",
    '6:6': "A withered right hand — the working hand, the man's living. Luke the physician notes which one.",
    '6:7': "They watch him to find an accusation. Their attention to the sabbath is really attention to him.",
    '6:8': "He puts the man in the middle of the room. The healing will not be done quietly to spare anyone.",
    '6:9': "To withhold good on the sabbath is not neutrality. Doing nothing is one of the options he names as evil.",
    '6:10': "The man stretches out the hand he cannot stretch out, and it is restored as he obeys.",
    '6:11': "They are filled with madness at a healing and begin plotting. Religion offended is a dangerous thing.",
    '6:12': "A whole night in prayer before choosing twelve men. Luke puts the prayer before the decision.",
    '6:16': "Judas Iscariot is named in the list, \"which also was the traitor.\" He was genuinely chosen.",
    '6:17': "He comes down and stands in the plain. The sermon is preached on the same level as the crowd.",
    '6:19': "The whole multitude sought to touch him, and virtue went out of him. He is not depleted by being drawn on.",
    '6:20': "\"Blessed be ye poor\" — not poor in spirit here. Luke leaves the blessing on actual empty hands.",
    '6:21': "Hunger and weeping now, filling and laughter then. The reversal is promised, not the present relief.",
    '6:22': "Exclusion, insult and a ruined reputation are listed as blessings when they come for his sake.",
    '6:23': "\"In the like manner did their fathers unto the prophets.\" Being hated puts you in respectable company.",
    '6:24': "\"Ye have received your consolation.\" The woe is not that comfort came, but that it already arrived in full.",
    '6:25': "The full and the laughing are warned, not the wicked. Satisfaction is the condition under threat.",
    '6:26': "Universal approval is listed with the woes. The false prophets were the popular ones.",
    '6:27': "\"Love your enemies\" is addressed to those which hear. It is not a strategy; it is an order.",
    '6:28': "Blessing and prayer are aimed at the people actively mistreating you. The enemy is prayed for by name.",
    '6:29': "Cheek and cloak and coat: he asks for more to be given than was taken.",
    '6:30': "\"Of him that taketh away thy goods ask them not again.\" Even recovering your own is let go.",
    '6:31': "The rule is stated positively: not refraining from harm but doing what you would want done.",
    '6:32': "\"What thank have ye?\" Reciprocal love is common practice, and he refuses to call it righteousness.",
    '6:34': "Lending on expectation of return is banking. He is asking for something with no recovery built in.",
    '6:35': "\"He is kind unto the unthankful and to the evil.\" The pattern for our love is God's treatment of his enemies.",
    '6:36': "Mercy is commanded as imitation: as your Father is merciful. The standard is his character, not a quota.",
    '6:37': "Judge, condemn, forgive — the same measure comes back. The verse is about what you hand out, not what you deserve.",
    '6:38': "\"Pressed down, and shaken together, and running over.\" The return is described as embarrassingly excessive.",
    '6:39': "Blind guides take the ditch with them. Teaching is not a private risk.",
    '6:40': "The disciple ends up like his master. Whoever teaches you is shaping what you will become.",
    '6:41': "The mote and the beam: the smaller fault is visible because it is in somebody else's eye.",
    '6:42': "The beam is not removed so you can stop looking, but so you can see clearly to help.",
    '6:43': "Good and corrupt trees are distinguished by nature before they are distinguished by fruit.",
    '6:44': "\"Every tree is known by his own fruit.\" Fruit is evidence of what a thing is, never the cause of it.",
    '6:45': "\"Of the abundance of the heart his mouth speaketh.\" Speech leaks what was already stored.",
    '6:46': "\"Why call ye me, Lord, Lord, and do not the things which I say?\" The title is contradicted by the life.",
    '6:48': "The digging deep is the whole difference, and it is the part nobody can see.",
    '6:49': "Both houses stood until the flood came. The storm did not create the difference; it revealed it.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 7 ----
    '7:2': "A centurion's servant is \"dear unto him.\" An officer of the occupying army cares whether a slave lives.",
    '7:3': "He sends Jewish elders rather than come himself. The Gentile knows how he is likely to be received.",
    '7:5': "His credentials are a building project and goodwill toward the nation. That is the case they can make.",
    '7:6': "\"I am not worthy that thou shouldest enter under my roof.\" He contradicts his own character witnesses.",
    '7:7': "\"Say in a word.\" He asks for the word alone and expects it to be enough, with no ceremony attached.",
    '7:8': "He reasons from his own chain of command: authority works by speaking, and Jesus outranks the illness.",
    '7:9': "Jesus marvels, and at a Gentile. The greatest faith in the book is found outside Israel.",
    '7:12': "An only son, and his mother a widow. Luke names both losses, because the second is the one that ruins her.",
    '7:13': "\"When the Lord saw her.\" Nobody asked him for anything; the compassion begins with what he notices.",
    '7:14': "He touches the bier, which defiles, and the procession stops. Death yields rather than spreads.",
    '7:15': "\"He delivered him to his mother.\" The miracle ends by handing a son back, not by drawing a crowd.",
    '7:16': "\"God hath visited his people.\" They reach for the old word for God coming near, and they are right.",
    '7:19': "\"Art thou he that should come?\" The forerunner, in prison, asks whether he read the signs correctly.",
    '7:22': "The list ends with the poor hearing the gospel, as though that were the greatest of the wonders.",
    '7:23': "\"Blessed is he, whosoever shall not be offended in me.\" A word of comfort to a man in a cell, with no rescue attached.",
    '7:24': "\"A reed shaken with the wind?\" He praises John to the crowd only after John's doubt has been aired.",
    '7:25': "Soft clothing belongs in kings' courts. John's wilderness dress was itself a verdict on Herod's palace.",
    '7:26': "\"Much more than a prophet.\" The man who just wavered is given the highest commendation in the chapter.",
    '7:28': "The least in the kingdom is greater than the greatest man born of women. Standing is not achievement.",
    '7:29': "The people and the publicans \"justified God\" by being baptized. Submitting to the verdict is how God is honoured.",
    '7:30': "The Pharisees rejected God's counsel \"against themselves.\" Refusing grace harms only the one refusing.",
    '7:32': "Children in the marketplace, cross whichever tune is played. The complaint was never about John's diet.",
    '7:33': "John fasted and was called demon-possessed. Austerity was not the objection.",
    '7:34': "\"A friend of publicans and sinners\" was meant as a slander. It is the most accurate thing they said about him.",
    '7:35': "\"Wisdom is justified of all her children.\" The proof is in the people it produces, not in the arguments.",
    '7:36': "He accepts a Pharisee's dinner invitation. He eats with the critics as readily as with the tax collectors.",
    '7:37': "A woman known in the city as a sinner walks into a Pharisee's dining room uninvited.",
    '7:38': "Tears, hair, kisses, ointment — the extravagance is what makes the room uncomfortable, and she does not stop.",
    '7:39': "Simon's objection is silent, and about what a prophet would have known. Jesus answers the thought.",
    '7:41': "Five hundred pence or fifty: both debtors are equally unable to pay. The gap between them stops mattering.",
    '7:42': "\"When they had nothing to pay, he frankly forgave them both.\" The cancellation is free and total.",
    '7:43': "Simon answers his own case correctly without noticing he is in it.",
    '7:44': "\"Seest thou this woman?\" Simon had looked at her all evening and seen only a category.",
    '7:45': "The host skipped the ordinary courtesies; the woman has not stopped. The contrast is in the basics.",
    '7:46': "No oil for the guest's head, and ointment poured on his feet. Respectability did less than disgrace did.",
    '7:47': "The parable runs the other way from the sentence: much love follows much forgiveness, and proves it.",
    '7:48': "\"Thy sins are forgiven.\" She is told directly what Simon had been told about her in the third person.",
    '7:49': "The guests ask who this is that forgives sins. The question the meal raises is not about her at all.",
    '7:50': "\"Thy faith hath saved thee; go in peace.\" Not her tears or her ointment — her faith, and she is sent out free.",

    # ---- Chapter 8 ----
    '8:2': "Mary Magdalene, out of whom went seven devils, is named first among the women who travelled with him.",
    '8:3': "Joanna, Susanna and others funded the ministry \"of their substance.\" Women paid for the work.",
    '8:5': "The sower scatters on the path as well as the field. The waste is built into the method.",
    '8:6': "Rock ground grows the fastest and dies first. Speed of response proves nothing about root.",
    '8:7': "The thorns spring up with it. Nothing is added to the field; what was already there does the choking.",
    '8:8': "The hundredfold comes from the ground, not from a better seed. The seed was the same everywhere.",
    '8:10': "The parables both reveal and conceal. Hearing without understanding is itself described as a judgment.",
    '8:12': "The devil's work is removal, \"lest they should believe and be saved.\" The word is taken, not disproved.",
    '8:13': "Received with joy, no root, gone under temptation. Joy at the start is not the test.",
    '8:14': "Cares, riches and pleasures do the choking. None of the three is a scandal; they are ordinary comforts.",
    '8:15': "\"Bring forth fruit with patience.\" Luke alone adds the patience, which is the part nobody wants.",
    '8:16': "A lighted candle is for the people entering the room, not for the one holding it.",
    '8:17': "Nothing hidden stays hidden. What is said in private is being said on the record.",
    '8:18': "\"Take heed how ye hear.\" Not merely what you hear — the manner of listening decides what you keep.",
    '8:19': "His mother and brethren cannot reach him for the crowd, which is where the whole scene starts.",
    '8:21': "Family is redefined around hearing the word and doing it. Even his mother is included on those terms.",
    '8:22': "\"Let us go over unto the other side.\" The storm comes in the middle of obeying his own instruction.",
    '8:23': "He fell asleep. The only untroubled person on the boat is the one with the most to lose.",
    '8:24': "\"Master, master, we perish.\" The panic is honest, and he answers it before he corrects it.",
    '8:25': "\"Where is your faith?\" They had faith enough to wake him, which he does not count as faith.",
    '8:27': "No clothes, no house, living in the tombs. Luke lists what the man has lost before naming the cause.",
    '8:28': "The demon calls him Son of God most high and begs not to be tormented. It knows the outcome already.",
    '8:29': "Chains and fetters had been tried. Restraint managed the symptoms and never touched the man.",
    '8:30': "\"What is thy name?\" The answer, Legion, is the first thing said about how much he had lost.",
    '8:33': "The pigs drown. The cost of one man's freedom is charged to the local economy.",
    '8:35': "Clothed, seated at Jesus' feet, in his right mind — and the neighbours were afraid.",
    '8:37': "They ask him to leave. A restored man was less welcome than a demoniac and a herd of swine.",
    '8:38': "The one man who wants to stay with Jesus is the one told to go.",
    '8:39': "Sent home to tell what God had done, he publishes what Jesus had done. Luke lets the shift stand.",
    '8:41': "A ruler of the synagogue falls at his feet. Office is no protection against a dying child.",
    '8:42': "One only daughter, twelve years old, and the crowd presses in anyway. The urgency belongs to Jairus alone.",
    '8:43': "Twelve years of bleeding and all her living spent on physicians. She is poor because she was sick.",
    '8:44': "She touches the border of his garment from behind, having planned to take healing without being seen.",
    '8:45': "\"Who touched me?\" Peter thinks the question absurd in a crowd, which is exactly the point of it.",
    '8:46': "He knows something has gone out of him. The healing was not automatic or impersonal.",
    '8:47': "She has to say out loud, before everyone, what she had spent twelve years hiding.",
    '8:48': "\"Daughter.\" An unclean woman is given a place in the family before she is dismissed in peace.",
    '8:49': "\"Thy daughter is dead; trouble not the Master.\" The delay that saved one woman has cost a girl her life.",
    '8:50': "\"Fear not: believe only.\" Faith is asked for after the news has already arrived.",
    '8:52': "\"She is not dead, but sleepeth.\" He renames death in the presence of people who know better.",
    '8:53': "They laughed him to scorn. The professional mourners were certain, and they were not wrong about the facts.",
    '8:54': "He takes her hand — touching a corpse again — and speaks two words to her.",
    '8:55': "\"He commanded to give her meat.\" The resurrection ends with somebody being told to fetch food.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 9 ----
    '9:1': "The authority is given, not developed. They have nothing of their own to bring to the assignment.",
    '9:2': "Preaching and healing go out together. The word and the mercy are one errand, not two departments.",
    '9:3': "No bread, no money, no spare coat. The mission is designed to leave them dependent on hospitality.",
    '9:4': "\"There abide\" — they are not to trade up to a better house once they have arrived.",
    '9:5': "Shaking off the dust ends the matter. They are not told to argue, stay, or try harder.",
    '9:7': "Herod is perplexed. Luke keeps returning to the man who has power and no idea what is happening.",
    '9:9': "\"John have I beheaded: but who is this?\" Curiosity from a man who has already shown what he does with prophets.",
    '9:12': "\"Send the multitude away.\" The disciples' solution is dispersal; it is reasonable and it is refused.",
    '9:13': "\"Give ye them to eat.\" The order is given to men who have just finished explaining they cannot.",
    '9:14': "Seated by fifties. The miracle is organised, and the disciples are made to do the arranging.",
    '9:16': "He blesses, breaks and gives to the disciples to distribute. It multiplies in their hands, not his.",
    '9:17': "Twelve baskets left over, one for each man who said there was not enough.",
    '9:18': "\"As he was alone praying, his disciples were with him.\" The great confession begins in prayer.",
    '9:20': "\"But whom say ye that I am?\" The question turns from public opinion to the men who have watched him closest.",
    '9:21': "He forbids them to publish the right answer. The title would be misread before the cross explains it.",
    '9:22': "\"The Son of man must suffer.\" Must — the cross is presented as necessity, not accident or defeat.",
    '9:23': "\"Daily\" is Luke's addition. The cross is not a single heroic hour but the shape of ordinary days.",
    '9:24': "Saving your life is how you lose it. The instinct for self-preservation is the thing that destroys.",
    '9:25': "The whole world is priced against one soul, and the exchange is called a bad bargain.",
    '9:26': "Shame works both directions. What is disowned now will be disowned then.",
    '9:28': "He goes up the mountain to pray, and takes three men with him. The glory happens during prayer.",
    '9:29': "His countenance is altered as he prays. The transfiguration is not staged for the audience below.",
    '9:31': "Moses and Elias speak of his decease \"which he should accomplish.\" His death is an achievement, not a fate.",
    '9:32': "They were heavy with sleep and nearly missed it. The disciples sleep through glory as they will through grief.",
    '9:33': "\"Not knowing what he said.\" Peter wants to build and stay; the voice will tell him to listen instead.",
    '9:34': "They were afraid as they entered the cloud. Entering God's presence is not described as comfortable.",
    '9:35': "\"This is my beloved Son: hear him.\" The command is to listen, which is the one thing Peter was not doing.",
    '9:38': "\"He is mine only child.\" Luke notices again what the loss of one son does to a household.",
    '9:40': "\"I besought thy disciples, and they could not.\" The failure is public and the father says so.",
    '9:41': "The rebuke lands on the generation, and then, without pause, \"Bring thy son hither.\"",
    '9:42': "The demon does its worst on the way, and the boy is delivered back to his father.",
    '9:43': "In the middle of the applause he turns to the disciples. The crowd's wonder is exactly when he warns them.",
    '9:44': "\"Let these sayings sink down into your ears\" — said while everyone around is marvelling at his power.",
    '9:45': "They did not understand and feared to ask. Not asking is how the misunderstanding was preserved.",
    '9:46': "A dispute about who is greatest, immediately after a prediction of his betrayal.",
    '9:47': "He answers an argument about rank by standing a child beside him. No speech would have been sharper.",
    '9:48': "\"He that is least among you all, the same shall be great.\" Greatness is redefined rather than distributed.",
    '9:49': "John reports stopping a man who was casting out devils, and expects to be commended.",
    '9:50': "\"Forbid him not.\" The criterion is not whether he follows with us but whether he is against us.",
    '9:51': "\"He stedfastly set his face to go to Jerusalem.\" The whole middle of Luke hangs on this sentence.",
    '9:53': "The Samaritans refuse him because of where he is headed. Old hostilities close the door.",
    '9:54': "James and John want fire from heaven, and they have a prophet's precedent ready to cite.",
    '9:55': "\"Ye know not what manner of spirit ye are of.\" Zeal for Christ can be entirely foreign to him.",
    '9:56': "He goes to another village. Rejection is met with a change of route, not a punishment.",
    '9:57': "\"Whithersoever thou goest\" is volunteered freely, before anyone has explained where that is.",
    '9:58': "Foxes have holes; he has nowhere to lay his head. The enthusiast is told the terms up front.",
    '9:59': "\"Suffer me first.\" The request is pious and reasonable, and the word first is the problem.",
    '9:60': "\"Let the dead bury their dead.\" The hardest sentence in the chapter, and it is not softened.",
    '9:61': "Even the farewell at home is a delay. Elijah allowed it; Jesus does not.",
    '9:62': "Looking back while ploughing ruins the furrow. The unfitness is practical, not moral posturing.",

    # ---- Chapter 10 ----
    '10:1': "Seventy more are sent, two by two, into the places he intends to visit. They go ahead of him, not instead.",
    '10:2': "The shortage is labourers, not harvest, and the first response commanded is prayer.",
    '10:3': "\"As lambs among wolves.\" He names the odds before they set out, and sends them anyway.",
    '10:5': "The first word spoken in any house is peace, before anything is known about who lives there.",
    '10:6': "If peace is refused it returns to the one who offered it. Nothing is lost by the offer.",
    '10:7': "\"The labourer is worthy of his hire.\" They live off the gospel, and are not to shop for better lodging.",
    '10:9': "Heal first, then say the kingdom has come near. The healing is what makes the sentence intelligible.",
    '10:11': "The kingdom has come near even to the city that refuses it. Nearness is not the same as reception.",
    '10:12': "Sodom fares better than the town that heard and shrugged. Judgment is measured against opportunity.",
    '10:13': "Tyre and Sidon would have repented. The pagan cities are held up as the better comparison.",
    '10:15': "Capernaum was exalted to heaven by having him, and that is precisely what makes the fall so far.",
    '10:16': "\"He that heareth you heareth me.\" The messengers' word carries the sender's authority, not their own.",
    '10:17': "The seventy return delighted that the devils obeyed. Their joy is in what they could do.",
    '10:18': "\"I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.\" He locates their small victories inside a larger collapse.",
    '10:20': "\"Rather rejoice, because your names are written in heaven.\" Assurance rests in the register, not the results.",
    '10:21': "He rejoices that the wise missed it and babes received it, and calls that the Father's good pleasure.",
    '10:22': "The Father is known only as the Son reveals him. There is no route around the man saying this.",
    '10:23': "\"Blessed are the eyes which see.\" The privilege is in what is being witnessed, not in the witnesses.",
    '10:25': "\"What shall I do to inherit eternal life?\" An inheritance is not earned, and the question is built wrong.",
    '10:26': "\"How readest thou?\" He sends the expert back to the text rather than supplying an answer.",
    '10:27': "The lawyer recites the law flawlessly. Knowing the summary and keeping it are different problems.",
    '10:28': "\"This do, and thou shalt live.\" The law is given its full demand, and it is the demand that undoes him.",
    '10:29': "\"Willing to justify himself\" is the whole motive for the question that follows.",
    '10:30': "The man in the ditch is stripped, wounded and half dead — no name, no nation, nothing to identify him.",
    '10:31': "The priest saw him and crossed the road. The evasion is deliberate; he had to move to avoid him.",
    '10:32': "The Levite looked at him and then passed by. Looking closely made no difference at all.",
    '10:33': "The one who stops is the one the lawyer would have called an enemy and a heretic.",
    '10:34': "Oil, wine, his own beast, an inn. The mercy costs him time, supplies, transport and dignity.",
    '10:35': "Two pence and an open account. He binds himself to a stranger's bill before he knows the total.",
    '10:36': "The question is reversed: not who deserves my help, but who acted as a neighbour.",
    '10:37': "The lawyer cannot say the word Samaritan. \"He that shewed mercy\" is as close as he can get.",
    '10:38': "Martha receives him into her house. The welcome is hers, and Luke gives her the credit for it.",
    '10:39': "Mary sits at his feet, in the posture of a disciple. A woman takes the student's place.",
    '10:40': "Martha asks Jesus to take her side against her sister. The serving has soured into resentment.",
    '10:41': "\"Martha, Martha.\" The name is said twice, gently, before anything is corrected.",
    '10:42': "\"One thing is needful.\" The good part is receiving rather than producing, and it will not be taken away.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 11 ----
    '11:1': "They ask after watching him pray, not after hearing a lecture on prayer.",
    '11:2': "\"Our Father\" — the prayer opens with a relationship already granted, before anything is requested.",
    '11:3': "\"Day by day\" bread. The petition is deliberately small and keeps the asking daily.",
    '11:4': "Sins and debts in one breath: what is forgiven us is meant to keep moving outward.",
    '11:5': "The man at midnight is asking on behalf of a guest he cannot feed. The need is borrowed.",
    '11:7': "\"Trouble me not: the door is now shut.\" The refusal is entirely reasonable, and it does not hold.",
    '11:8': "Not friendship but shameless persistence gets the bread. The argument runs from the lesser case upward.",
    '11:9': "Ask, seek, knock — three verbs, all of them active, none of them requiring eloquence.",
    '11:10': "\"Every one that asketh receiveth.\" The promise is made without a screening of who is asking.",
    '11:11': "No father hands his hungry son a stone. The comparison assumes we already know what fathers are like.",
    '11:13': "\"Ye then, being evil\" is said in passing, as a settled fact, inside a promise of the Spirit.",
    '11:14': "The man's devil was dumb. What was taken from him was speech, and speech is what returns.",
    '11:15': "The same miracle produces worship in some and the charge of devilry in others.",
    '11:17': "A divided kingdom falls. He answers a slander with logic before he answers it with authority.",
    '11:19': "\"By whom do your sons cast them out?\" The accusation convicts their own exorcists first.",
    '11:20': "\"The finger of God.\" If the power is God's, then the kingdom has already arrived in the room.",
    '11:21': "The strong man's goods are at peace. Untroubled possession is not proof that all is well.",
    '11:22': "A stronger one strips the armour he trusted in. Deliverance is a robbery, not a negotiation.",
    '11:23': "There is no neutral ground here. Not gathering with him counts as scattering.",
    '11:25': "\"Swept and garnished\" and empty. A cleaned-up life with no new tenant is an invitation.",
    '11:26': "Seven worse spirits move into the tidy house. Reform without Christ leaves worse room than it found.",
    '11:27': "A woman blesses his mother. It is the warmest thing anyone says to him in the chapter.",
    '11:28': "\"Yea rather, blessed are they that hear the word of God, and keep it.\" Hearing outranks bloodline.",
    '11:29': "The demand for a sign is itself named as the evidence of an evil generation.",
    '11:30': "Jonah was the sign by being three days gone and then preaching. The pattern is stated before the cross.",
    '11:31': "A pagan queen travelled the known world for wisdom, and will stand as witness against the locals.",
    '11:32': "Nineveh repented under a reluctant prophet. \"A greater than Jonas is here\" and the town is unmoved.",
    '11:33': "Light is lit to be seen by those coming in. Concealment defeats the purpose of the lamp.",
    '11:34': "The eye decides whether the whole body is lit. What you attend to fills the rest of you.",
    '11:35': "\"Take heed that the light which is in thee be not darkness.\" The worst error is mistaken certainty.",
    '11:37': "He accepts the invitation of a man who will spend the meal disapproving of him.",
    '11:38': "The Pharisee marvels at an unwashed hand. The offence is a ritual, and it is felt as an outrage.",
    '11:39': "Clean cup, foul interior. The complaint is not that they polish the outside but that they stop there.",
    '11:40': "\"Did not he that made that which is without make that which is within also?\" God sees both sides of the dish.",
    '11:41': "Alms are prescribed where washing was expected. Mercy given away does what scrubbing could not.",
    '11:42': "Mint and rue tithed to the leaf, and judgment and the love of God passed over. Precision is not obedience.",
    '11:43': "The best seats and the greetings in the market. Religion had become a way of being seen.",
    '11:44': "Unmarked graves defile the people walking over them unawares. The damage is done to others.",
    '11:45': "\"Thou reproachest us also.\" The lawyer volunteers himself into the woes by objecting.",
    '11:46': "Burdens laid on other men's backs and not touched with a finger. Law is easy to impose and heavy to carry.",
    '11:47': "They build monuments to the prophets their fathers killed, and call that honouring them.",
    '11:48': "Building the tombs completes the work of the killing. The generations are in one trade.",
    '11:50': "The blood of all the prophets is required of this generation. Guilt accumulates rather than fades.",
    '11:51': "From Abel to Zacharias — the whole record of the killed righteous, named at the two ends.",
    '11:52': "\"Ye have taken away the key of knowledge.\" Not entering themselves, they blocked the doorway for others.",
    '11:54': "They lay in wait to catch something out of his mouth. Listening for evidence is not listening.",

    # ---- Chapter 12 ----
    '12:1': "With a crowd trampling one another to hear him, his first word to his own is a warning about hypocrisy.",
    '12:2': "Leaven works unseen, and so does exposure. Nothing covered stays covered.",
    '12:3': "What was whispered in a closet will be announced from a roof. Privacy is temporary.",
    '12:4': "\"My friends\" — the term of affection arrives in a sentence about being killed.",
    '12:5': "The only being worth fearing is the one whose authority outlasts death.",
    '12:6': "Five sparrows for two farthings, and not one forgotten. The cheapest thing in the market is remembered.",
    '12:7': "The hairs of your head are numbered — counted, not merely known. The attention is absurdly specific.",
    '12:8': "Confession before men is answered by confession before angels. The court above matches the court below.",
    '12:10': "A word against the Son of man is forgivable. The unforgivable sin is refusing the Spirit who forgives.",
    '12:11': "They are told not to prepare a defence. The one preparation forbidden is the one they would want.",
    '12:12': "\"In the same hour\" — the help arrives when it is needed, not in advance to be stored up.",
    '12:13': "A man interrupts teaching about persecution to ask for help with a family estate.",
    '12:14': "\"Who made me a judge or a divider over you?\" He declines the role everyone wants him to take.",
    '12:15': "\"A man's life consisteth not in the abundance of the things which he possesseth.\" Stated flatly, as a fact.",
    '12:16': "The land brought forth plentifully. The rich man's trouble begins with a genuine blessing.",
    '12:17': "\"What shall I do?\" He is not wicked in any way the neighbours could name. He is simply alone with his surplus.",
    '12:18': "Bigger barns are the obvious answer. Prudence and greed use identical vocabulary.",
    '12:19': "\"Soul, thou hast much goods laid up for many years.\" He talks only to himself, and the years are not his.",
    '12:20': "\"Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee.\" God speaks once in the parable, and it is at the end.",
    '12:21': "\"Rich toward God\" is set against treasure laid up for oneself. The alternative to hoarding is not poverty.",
    '12:22': "The anxiety he forbids is about food and clothing, not luxuries. The ordinary worry is the one addressed.",
    '12:24': "Ravens neither sow nor reap, and are unclean birds besides. God feeds them anyway.",
    '12:25': "Worry adds nothing, not even an inch. It is not merely wrong; it is ineffective.",
    '12:27': "Lilies toil not and outdress Solomon. Glory here is received rather than manufactured.",
    '12:28': "Grass is clothed and then burned. If God dresses what lasts a day, the argument is finished.",
    '12:30': "The nations chase these things; your Father already knows you need them. The difference is the Father.",
    '12:31': "\"Seek ye the kingdom of God; and all these things shall be added.\" Order of pursuit, not a trade of one for the other.",
    '12:32': "\"Fear not, little flock.\" The kingdom is given because it pleases the giver, not because the flock is impressive.",
    '12:33': "Bags that do not wear out. He redirects investment rather than condemning it.",
    '12:34': "\"Where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.\" The heart follows the money, not the other way round.",
    '12:35': "Girded loins and burning lamps: dressed for work and awake, which is the whole of readiness.",
    '12:36': "The servants wait to open the door \"immediately.\" Readiness is measured in how fast they answer.",
    '12:37': "The master girds himself and serves them. The reward for watching is being waited on by the Lord.",
    '12:39': "Nobody schedules a burglary. The comparison for his coming is deliberately unflattering.",
    '12:40': "\"At an hour when ye think not.\" The unexpectedness is the point, so no calculation will help.",
    '12:42': "The steward's job is giving the household its food in due season. Authority exists to feed people.",
    '12:44': "Faithfulness in a household is answered with rule over everything. The scale of the reward is out of proportion.",
    '12:45': "\"My lord delayeth his coming.\" The abuse of the servants starts as a conclusion about the timetable.",
    '12:46': "The master returns on a day the servant had ruled out. Assuming delay is how he is caught.",
    '12:47': "Knowing the will and not doing it is judged more severely than not knowing.",
    '12:48': "\"Unto whomsoever much is given, of him shall be much required.\" Privilege is measured as liability.",
    '12:49': "\"I am come to send fire on the earth.\" He does not describe his mission as soothing.",
    '12:50': "\"How am I straitened till it be accomplished.\" He calls his death a baptism and is under pressure until it comes.",
    '12:51': "\"Suppose ye that I am come to give peace on earth? I tell you, Nay.\" He forecloses the sentimental reading himself.",
    '12:52': "Five in one house, three against two. The division runs through the household, not between households.",
    '12:53': "Fathers, sons, mothers, daughters, in-laws — the split is named down to the relationship.",
    '12:54': "They read a westerly cloud and know rain is coming. The skill is real; the application is missing.",
    '12:56': "\"Ye can discern the face of the sky, but how is it that ye do not discern this time?\" Weather, yes; the hour, no.",
    '12:57': "\"Why even of yourselves judge ye not what is right?\" They are not short of information but of honesty.",
    '12:58': "Settle with your adversary on the road. The window for agreement closes at the courthouse door.",
    '12:59': "\"Till thou hast paid the very last mite.\" The law's account is exact and it does not forgive anything.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 13 ----
    '13:1': "News of Pilate's massacre is brought to him as a theological puzzle rather than a grief.",
    '13:2': "He refuses the assumption behind the report: that the murdered Galilaeans must have deserved it.",
    '13:3': "The question about other people's guilt is turned around into a call to repent yourself.",
    '13:4': "A tower falls on eighteen men. He raises the accident himself, so nobody can limit the point to politics.",
    '13:5': "The same warning repeated, and the repetition is the argument. Nothing is added; nothing is withdrawn.",
    '13:6': "The fig tree is planted in a vineyard, given the best ground, and produces nothing.",
    '13:7': "\"Why cumbereth it the ground?\" Three years of nothing, and the owner's verdict is entirely fair.",
    '13:8': "\"Let it alone this year also.\" Someone intervenes between the tree and the axe and asks for time.",
    '13:9': "The reprieve is real but dated. Mercy delays judgment here; it does not cancel it.",
    '13:11': "Eighteen years bowed together, unable to lift herself. Luke measures the affliction in years.",
    '13:13': "Made straight, she glorifies God. The first use of her recovered body is praise.",
    '13:14': "The ruler rebukes the crowd rather than Jesus, and cites the work week as though healing were labour.",
    '13:15': "They untie an ox on the sabbath without a thought. The animals were already exempt.",
    '13:16': "\"A daughter of Abraham\" — he gives her the standing the ruler never granted her.",
    '13:17': "His adversaries are ashamed and the people rejoice. Luke keeps both reactions in the same sentence.",
    '13:19': "A seed in a garden becomes a tree with birds in it. The kingdom is small at the point where you can see it.",
    '13:21': "Leaven is hidden in the meal by a woman, and works out of sight until everything is changed.",
    '13:23': "\"Are there few that be saved?\" A speculative question, and he answers a different one.",
    '13:24': "\"Strive to enter in at the strait gate.\" The question about numbers is redirected to the person asking.",
    '13:25': "Once the door is shut, knocking and calling Lord makes no difference. The timing was the thing.",
    '13:26': "\"We have eaten and drunk in thy presence.\" Proximity and religious memory are offered as credentials.",
    '13:27': "\"I know you not whence ye are.\" The relation is what is lacking, not the attendance record.",
    '13:28': "The patriarchs are inside and the natives of the house are outside. The shock is in who is where.",
    '13:29': "They come from all four directions and sit down. The kingdom's picture is a table with strangers at it.",
    '13:30': "\"There are last which shall be first.\" The order is not adjusted; it is turned over.",
    '13:31': "Pharisees warn him about Herod. Not every warning from an opponent is a trap.",
    '13:32': "\"Go ye, and tell that fox.\" He sends the tetrarch a message and keeps his own schedule.",
    '13:33': "\"It cannot be that a prophet perish out of Jerusalem.\" He names the city that will kill him as his destination.",
    '13:34': "\"As a hen doth gather her brood\" — and \"ye would not.\" The image of tenderness ends in refusal.",
    '13:35': "The house is left desolate, and the lament still ends in a blessing they will one day say.",

    # ---- Chapter 14 ----
    '14:1': "He eats in a chief Pharisee's house on the sabbath, and \"they watched him.\" The meal is a surveillance.",
    '14:2': "A man with dropsy stands in front of him at the sabbath dinner. Luke lets the coincidence look arranged.",
    '14:4': "\"They held their peace.\" Silence is safer than an answer, and he heals into that silence.",
    '14:5': "An ox in a pit would be pulled out at once. Their practice already contradicts their objection.",
    '14:7': "He watched the guests choosing the best seats. The parable comes from what he noticed at the door.",
    '14:9': "\"Begin with shame to take the lowest room.\" Self-promotion is answered by the host, not by rivals.",
    '14:10': "\"Friend, go up higher.\" Honour is given by the host; seizing it in advance forfeits it.",
    '14:11': "Both halves are passive: abased and exalted. Neither is something you do to yourself.",
    '14:12': "He turns from the guests to the host. Inviting those who can return the favour is trade, not hospitality.",
    '14:13': "\"Call the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind.\" The guest list is chosen for inability to reciprocate.",
    '14:14': "\"They cannot recompense thee\" is given as the reason for blessing, not as a drawback.",
    '14:15': "A guest offers a pious remark about eating bread in the kingdom, and gets a parable about refusing the invitation.",
    '14:16': "The supper is great and many are bidden. Everything is prepared before anyone is asked to come.",
    '14:17': "\"All things are now ready.\" The second call is not a request for help but an announcement that nothing is lacking.",
    '14:18': "The first excuse is a field already bought. The ground will still be there tomorrow.",
    '14:19': "Five yoke of oxen to inspect, after purchase. The business is real and the timing is a choice.",
    '14:20': "\"I have married a wife, and therefore I cannot come.\" The only excuse that does not even ask to be excused.",
    '14:21': "The master's anger fills the house with the people who were never on the list.",
    '14:22': "\"Yet there is room.\" The house is not full even after the poor are brought in.",
    '14:23': "\"Compel them to come in.\" The pressure falls on those who assume the invitation cannot be meant for them.",
    '14:25': "Great multitudes are following him, and he turns around to make it harder rather than easier.",
    '14:26': "\"Hate not his father, and mother\" — the comparison is absolute, and Luke does not soften it to \"love less.\"",
    '14:27': "Bearing a cross meant walking to your own execution. The image had not yet become jewellery.",
    '14:28': "Count the cost before laying the foundation. He wants followers who know the price in advance.",
    '14:30': "\"This man began to build, and was not able to finish.\" The mockery is at the abandonment, not the attempt.",
    '14:31': "Ten thousand against twenty thousand. The king who reckons honestly may conclude he cannot win.",
    '14:32': "The weaker king sues for peace while there is still distance. Surrender is the wise move, not the shameful one.",
    '14:33': "\"Forsaketh not all that he hath\" — the accounting ends with everything on the table.",
    '14:34': "Salt that has lost its savour cannot be re-salted. There is nothing else to season it with.",
    '14:35': "Useless for the field and the dunghill both. Failed discipleship is not neutral; it is thrown out.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 15 ----
    '15:1': "Publicans and sinners draw near to hear him. Whatever he was doing, it did not repel the disreputable.",
    '15:2': "\"This man receiveth sinners, and eateth with them.\" The complaint is accurate, and the three parables answer it.",
    '15:4': "The ninety and nine are left in the wilderness while he goes after one. The arithmetic is deliberately bad.",
    '15:5': "He carries it home on his shoulders. The sheep contributes nothing to its own recovery.",
    '15:6': "The finding ends in a party with the neighbours. Joy that stays private is not the point here.",
    '15:7': "More joy over one repentant sinner than over ninety-nine needing none — said to men who thought they were the ninety-nine.",
    '15:8': "The woman lights a candle and sweeps until she finds it. The searching is hers, and it is thorough.",
    '15:9': "She calls her friends over a single coin. The celebration is out of scale with the sum.",
    '15:10': "Joy \"in the presence of the angels of God.\" Heaven's reaction to one repentance is the measure offered.",
    '15:12': "Asking for the inheritance early is wishing the father dead, and the father hands it over.",
    '15:13': "A far country and riotous living. Luke gives the ruin four words and moves on.",
    '15:14': "He spent all, and then the famine came. The trouble arrives after the money is gone, not before.",
    '15:15': "A Jewish boy feeding pigs for a foreigner. He is as far from home as the story can put him.",
    '15:16': "\"No man gave unto him.\" The far country's generosity runs out exactly when he has nothing to spend.",
    '15:17': "\"When he came to himself.\" Repentance begins as hunger and a clear-eyed memory of home.",
    '15:18': "The speech he rehearses is true, and he prepares it while still in the pigsty.",
    '15:19': "\"Make me as one of thy hired servants.\" He plans to negotiate a lesser standing he will never be allowed.",
    '15:20': "The father was watching, and ran. An old man ran, which no dignified man did, and he ran first.",
    '15:21': "The son delivers his confession and never reaches the part about being hired. He is cut off by an embrace.",
    '15:22': "Robe, ring and shoes: restored as a son, not hired as a servant. The status is given back whole.",
    '15:23': "The fatted calf is killed. There is no probation and no repayment schedule.",
    '15:24': "\"Was dead, and is alive again.\" The father describes the return in the language of resurrection.",
    '15:25': "The elder son is out in the field, working, when the music starts. He hears the joy from outside.",
    '15:28': "He would not go in, and the father came out to him too. Both sons are gone after.",
    '15:29': "\"These many years do I serve thee.\" He describes life in his father's house as servitude, and never noticed.",
    '15:30': "\"This thy son\" — he will not say brother. The distance he keeps is his own doing.",
    '15:31': "\"Thou art ever with me, and all that I have is thine.\" He had everything and enjoyed none of it.",
    '15:32': "The father corrects the pronoun: \"this thy brother.\" The parable ends without telling us whether he went in.",

    # ---- Chapter 16 ----
    '16:1': "The steward is accused of wasting goods, and the parable never says the accusation was false.",
    '16:3': "\"I cannot dig; to beg I am ashamed.\" He knows exactly what he is and is not willing to do.",
    '16:4': "His whole plan is about who will take him in afterward. He thinks past the dismissal to the day after.",
    '16:6': "Fifty for a hundred, written in the debtor's own hand. The forgiveness is real, and it is not his to give.",
    '16:8': "The lord commends the shrewdness, not the theft. The children of this world plan for the future and we do not.",
    '16:9': "Use money to make friends who outlast it. Wealth is treated as a tool with an expiry date.",
    '16:10': "Faithfulness in small things is not preparation for the big things; it is the same thing at a different scale.",
    '16:11': "Money is called the unrighteous and lesser trust. What we handle badly here reveals what we would do with more.",
    '16:12': "It is all another man's in the end. Nothing in a steward's hands was ever his own.",
    '16:13': "Not \"should not\" but cannot. Two masters is not a difficult arrangement; it is an impossible one.",
    '16:14': "The covetous Pharisees derided him. Ridicule is the usual answer when the subject is one's own money.",
    '16:15': "What men esteem highly is abomination in God's sight. The two scales are not different; they are opposed.",
    '16:16': "The law and the prophets ran until John. Something changed at that point, and the change is not a cancellation.",
    '16:17': "Not a tittle of the law will fail. Grace does not dismiss the demand; it meets it.",
    '16:18': "Between two sentences about the law's permanence, he tightens the marriage law rather than relaxing it.",
    '16:19': "Purple, fine linen, and a feast every day. The rich man is never accused of a crime.",
    '16:20': "The beggar has a name and the rich man does not. Luke reverses whose identity mattered.",
    '16:21': "He lay at the gate, close enough to see the crumbs. The dogs attended to him and the household did not.",
    '16:22': "One is carried by angels; the other \"was buried,\" which was the more impressive event at the time.",
    '16:23': "He lifts his eyes in torment and can still see Abraham afar off. The distance is visible from one side.",
    '16:24': "He asks for Lazarus by name, and still treats him as a servant to be sent on errands.",
    '16:25': "\"Son, remember.\" The answer is not a rebuke but an accounting of what each man already received.",
    '16:26': "\"A great gulf fixed\" — the separation is not maintained by anger but by finality.",
    '16:29': "\"They have Moses and the prophets; let them hear them.\" The scriptures they already own are enough.",
    '16:30': "\"If one went unto them from the dead, they will repent.\" He is sure a miracle would do what the word did not.",
    '16:31': "\"Neither will they be persuaded, though one rose from the dead.\" Luke writes this knowing how the book ends.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 17 ----
    '17:1': "Offences are called inevitable, and the woe still falls on whoever brings them. Inevitability excuses nobody.",
    '17:2': "A millstone and the sea are preferred to harming one little one. The comparison is not a figure of speech.",
    '17:3': "Rebuke and forgive are held together. Neither the confrontation nor the pardon is optional.",
    '17:4': "Seven times in one day, with the same words each time. The suspicion that he does not mean it is not grounds for refusing.",
    '17:5': "\"Increase our faith.\" The apostles ask for more capacity after being told to forgive repeatedly.",
    '17:6': "Faith the size of a mustard seed is enough. The request for more misunderstands what faith does.",
    '17:8': "The servant comes in from the field and cooks the master's supper. Nobody in the story finds this unfair.",
    '17:9': "\"Doth he thank that servant?\" Duty done is not a favour conferred.",
    '17:10': "\"We are unprofitable servants.\" Even complete obedience earns nothing; there was never a surplus to bank.",
    '17:12': "Ten lepers stand afar off, as the law required. Even their asking has to be shouted from a distance.",
    '17:14': "They are cleansed as they went. The healing comes after they start obeying, not before.",
    '17:15': "One turns back when he notices. The other nine were doing exactly what they were told.",
    '17:16': "\"And he was a Samaritan.\" Luke holds the detail to the end of the sentence, where it does the most work.",
    '17:17': "\"Where are the nine?\" The question is left unanswered. Gratitude was the rarest thing in the village.",
    '17:18': "\"Save this stranger.\" The outsider is the only one who came back, and the word stranger is not softened.",
    '17:19': "Nine were cleansed; this one is made whole. Something was given to him that the healing alone did not carry.",
    '17:20': "\"The kingdom of God cometh not with observation.\" It will not be detected by watching for it.",
    '17:21': "Not here or there, but among them already. The kingdom was standing in front of the men asking when it would come.",
    '17:23': "\"Go not after them.\" Announcements of a located Christ are to be ignored on principle.",
    '17:24': "Lightning across the whole sky: when it happens, nobody will need to be informed of it.",
    '17:25': "\"But first must he suffer.\" The glory is real, and the cross stands between here and it.",
    '17:26': "Noah's generation is the comparison, and what is described is not wickedness but ordinary life.",
    '17:27': "Eating, drinking, marrying — the things named are innocent. The fault was noticing nothing.",
    '17:28': "Buying, selling, planting, building. Sodom's last day looked like a good quarter.",
    '17:29': "\"The same day that Lot went out\" — the reprieve and the ruin land on the same date.",
    '17:31': "Nobody goes back down for his belongings. The things in the house are not worth the trip.",
    '17:32': "\"Remember Lot's wife.\" Three words, and the shortest verse in the gospel is a warning about looking back.",
    '17:33': "Seeking to save your life is how you lose it. The sentence appears again because we do not believe it once.",
    '17:34': "Two in one bed, and only one is taken. The division runs between people who are as close as people get.",
    '17:35': "Two women at the same mill, doing the same work, and separated. Shared labour is not shared standing.",
    '17:37': "\"Where, Lord?\" They want a location, and the answer about eagles and a body refuses to give one.",

    # ---- Chapter 18 ----
    '18:1': "Luke states the point before the parable: pray always and do not faint. The alternative to praying is giving up.",
    '18:2': "A judge who feared neither God nor man. The parable's picture of God is deliberately the wrong one.",
    '18:3': "A widow with no money, no advocate and no leverage, asking the one man who can help and will not.",
    '18:5': "\"Lest by her continual coming she weary me.\" He acts from annoyance, and the widow gets justice anyway.",
    '18:6': "\"Hear what the unjust judge saith.\" We are told to listen carefully to the worst character in the story.",
    '18:7': "If persistence moves a corrupt judge, how much more the Father who chose his own and hears them night and day.",
    '18:8': "\"Shall he find faith on the earth?\" The parable about praying ends with a question about whether anyone still is.",
    '18:9': "Trusting in themselves and despising others. The two halves of that sentence always travel together.",
    '18:10': "Two men, the same temple, the same hour. Everything external about their praying is identical.",
    '18:11': "He \"prayed thus with himself.\" The prayer is addressed to God and audited by the man praying it.",
    '18:12': "Fasting twice a week and tithing everything exceeds what the law required. He is not lying about any of it.",
    '18:13': "\"God be merciful to me a sinner.\" Seven words, no comparison with anyone, and nothing offered in hand.",
    '18:14': "\"This man went down to his house justified.\" The verdict is given on the spot and on no basis he supplied.",
    '18:15': "Infants are brought to be touched, and the disciples rebuke the parents for wasting his time.",
    '18:16': "\"Of such is the kingdom of God.\" The qualification is having nothing to bring, which infants have perfectly.",
    '18:17': "Receiving it as a child means receiving, not achieving. The kingdom is taken as a gift or not at all.",
    '18:18': "A ruler asks what he must do to inherit. He is rich, respected, and asking the wrong verb.",
    '18:19': "\"Why callest thou me good?\" The compliment is examined rather than accepted, and it sets a standard he cannot meet.",
    '18:21': "\"All these have I kept from my youth up.\" He says it without hesitation, and is not contradicted.",
    '18:22': "\"Yet lackest thou one thing.\" The one thing turns out to be everything he owns.",
    '18:23': "He was very sorrowful, for he was very rich. The wealth made the sorrow, not the command.",
    '18:24': "\"How hardly shall they that have riches enter.\" Said sadly, watching a good man walk away.",
    '18:25': "The camel and the needle's eye is not a narrow gate in a wall. It is an impossibility, stated as one.",
    '18:26': "\"Who then can be saved?\" The hearers assume wealth was evidence of God's favour, so the question is honest.",
    '18:27': "\"The things which are impossible with men are possible with God.\" Salvation is moved out of human hands entirely.",
    '18:28': "\"Lo, we have left all.\" Peter begins the accounting almost immediately, as we all would.",
    '18:30': "Manifold more in this present time, and life everlasting after it. The compensation begins before the end.",
    '18:31': "\"Behold, we go up to Jerusalem.\" He tells them plainly where the road ends and keeps walking it.",
    '18:32': "Mocked, spitefully entreated, spat upon. The indignities are listed before the death, as if they mattered too.",
    '18:34': "They understood none of it. Three clear predictions and the meaning is still hidden from them.",
    '18:35': "A blind beggar by the roadside outside Jericho, in the position he occupies every day.",
    '18:38': "\"Thou son of David\" — the blind man uses the title the seeing crowd has not worked out.",
    '18:39': "They rebuke him and he shouts louder. The crowd around Jesus is the obstacle between him and Jesus.",
    '18:40': "Jesus stood still and had him brought. The procession to Jerusalem stops for one beggar.",
    '18:41': "\"What wilt thou that I shall do unto thee?\" He is asked to name it rather than assumed to be understood.",
    '18:42': "\"Receive thy sight.\" The word and the seeing arrive together, and his faith is what is commended.",
    '18:43': "He follows him, glorifying God, and the crowd that told him to be quiet joins the praise.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 19 ----
    '19:2': "Chief among the publicans, and rich. Luke names the profession and the proceeds in one breath.",
    '19:3': "He wanted to see who Jesus was, and the crowd and his own height were in the way.",
    '19:4': "A wealthy man climbs a tree in public. He gives up his dignity before he gives up anything else.",
    '19:5': "\"To day I must abide at thy house.\" He is called down by name, and the visit is announced, not requested.",
    '19:7': "\"Gone to be guest with a man that is a sinner.\" They all murmured, and they were all correct.",
    '19:8': "The restitution comes after the welcome, not as its condition. Fourfold is more than the law required.",
    '19:9': "\"This day is salvation come to this house.\" It arrived with a person and stayed for dinner.",
    '19:10': "\"To seek and to save that which was lost.\" The mission statement is given in a tax collector's dining room.",
    '19:11': "The parable is told because they expected the kingdom to appear immediately. It is a correction of the calendar.",
    '19:13': "\"Occupy till I come.\" The interval is for work, and the work is ordinary trading.",
    '19:14': "\"We will not have this man to reign over us.\" The citizens' message runs alongside the servants' assignment.",
    '19:16': "\"Thy pound hath gained ten pounds.\" The servant credits the pound, not himself.",
    '19:17': "Faithful in a very little, given authority over ten cities. The reward bears no relation to the sum.",
    '19:20': "The pound comes back intact in a napkin. Nothing was lost, and that is the whole failure.",
    '19:21': "\"I feared thee, because thou art an austere man.\" A wrong picture of the master produced a buried pound.",
    '19:22': "\"Out of thine own mouth will I judge thee.\" He is held to the master he claimed to believe in.",
    '19:23': "Even the bank would have done. The demand was not brilliance but the smallest use of what was given.",
    '19:26': "What is unused is taken away. Gifts left in a napkin do not stay neutral.",
    '19:27': "The parable ends with the slaughter of the citizens who refused him. Luke does not soften the close.",
    '19:30': "A colt on which no man has sat: unbroken, unused, and that is what qualifies it.",
    '19:31': "\"Because the Lord hath need of him.\" The only explanation offered to the owners is a claim of need.",
    '19:33': "The owners ask the obvious question, and the disciples have nothing to give them but the sentence they were taught.",
    '19:35': "They set Jesus on the colt over their own garments. The royal entry is improvised out of what people are wearing.",
    '19:37': "They praise God \"for all the mighty works that they had seen.\" The joy is grounded in what they witnessed.",
    '19:38': "\"Peace in heaven, and glory in the highest\" — Luke echoes the angels at the birth, here at the end of the road.",
    '19:39': "The Pharisees ask him to silence the praise. Even the acclamation is treated as a public order problem.",
    '19:40': "\"The stones would immediately cry out.\" The confession will be made; only the speakers are negotiable.",
    '19:41': "He wept over the city. The triumphal entry has him crying in the middle of the cheering.",
    '19:42': "\"Now they are hid from thine eyes.\" There is a point past which the things belonging to peace cannot be seen.",
    '19:43': "The siege is described in engineering detail, decades before it happened.",
    '19:44': "\"Because thou knewest not the time of thy visitation.\" The charge is not cruelty or idolatry but missing the day.",
    '19:45': "The first thing he does in the temple is empty it of commerce.",
    '19:46': "\"My house is the house of prayer.\" The trade did not stop worship; it stood exactly where prayer should have been.",
    '19:47': "He taught daily in the temple while the authorities looked for a way to kill him. Both things are ordinary now.",
    '19:48': "The people hung on his words, and that popularity is the only thing keeping him alive for the moment.",

    # ---- Chapter 20 ----
    '20:2': "\"By what authority doest thou these things?\" The question is fair, and they have already decided the answer.",
    '20:4': "\"The baptism of John, was it from heaven, or of men?\" He answers a question about authority with one.",
    '20:5': "They calculate consequences in both directions and never once ask what is true.",
    '20:6': "Fear of stoning decides their theology. The people's opinion is the only factor weighed.",
    '20:7': "\"They could not tell.\" A professional judgment withheld to avoid the cost of making it.",
    '20:8': "He declines to answer men who have shown they will not accept an answer.",
    '20:9': "A vineyard let out to husbandmen. Everything in the parable was somebody else's before it was theirs.",
    '20:12': "A third is wounded and thrown out. The escalation is met with more sending, not with force.",
    '20:13': "\"It may be they will reverence him.\" The owner sends his beloved son into a place that has already proven lethal.",
    '20:14': "\"This is the heir: come, let us kill him.\" They know exactly who he is; that is why they kill him.",
    '20:15': "Cast out of the vineyard and killed. Luke's order matches the crucifixion outside the city wall.",
    '20:16': "\"God forbid\" is the crowd's reaction to the verdict, not to the murder.",
    '20:17': "The rejected stone becomes the head of the corner. The builders' judgment is overturned, not appealed.",
    '20:18': "Falling on the stone or being fallen on: the only options offered are broken or ground to powder.",
    '20:19': "\"They perceived that he had spoken this parable against them.\" They understood it perfectly and changed nothing.",
    '20:20': "Spies who \"feign themselves just men.\" The trap is baited with sincerity.",
    '20:21': "The flattery is entirely accurate, which is what makes it flattery.",
    '20:22': "The tribute question has no safe answer: Rome or the crowd will be offended either way.",
    '20:23': "\"He perceived their craftiness.\" The question is answered as a question and exposed as a trap.",
    '20:24': "\"Whose image and superscription hath it?\" They produce the coin themselves, having said they objected to it.",
    '20:25': "Caesar's image on the coin, God's image on the man. The division of what is owed is not equal.",
    '20:26': "They marvelled and held their peace. The trap closed on nothing.",
    '20:27': "The Sadducees deny the resurrection and arrive with a riddle about it. The question is not a question.",
    '20:28': "They quote Moses on levirate marriage, using the law about protecting widows to make a joke.",
    '20:33': "\"Whose wife of them is she?\" Seven husbands and no children, and the woman herself is not considered.",
    '20:34': "\"The children of this world marry.\" He grants marriage its full place here before speaking of there.",
    '20:35': "The next world is not this one extended. Its arrangements are not ours with the problems removed.",
    '20:36': "\"Neither can they die any more.\" Death is what necessitated the whole legal machinery of the riddle.",
    '20:37': "Moses at the bush is the proof text, chosen because the Sadducees accepted only Moses.",
    '20:38': "\"He is not a God of the dead, but of the living.\" The patriarchs' God still uses the present tense about them.",
    '20:40': "\"They durst not ask him any question at all.\" The interrogation ends because the questions ran out.",
    '20:41': "Now he asks the question. Having answered them all, he puts one about himself.",
    '20:42': "David calls him Lord in the psalm, which is not how a man speaks of his own descendant.",
    '20:44': "\"How is he then his son?\" The puzzle is left standing, and it can only be solved by the incarnation.",
    '20:46': "Long robes, greetings, best seats, chief rooms. The list is about being seen, four times over.",
    '20:47': "Devouring widows' houses and making long prayers are named in a single breath, as one practice.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 21 ----
    '21:1': "He looked up and watched people give. The offering box was under observation the whole time.",
    '21:2': "Two mites, the smallest coins in circulation. She had two and could have kept one.",
    '21:3': "\"More than they all\" — the accounting is done by what is left over, not by what is handed in.",
    '21:4': "Abundance and penury are the two categories. Proportion, not sum, is what he weighs.",
    '21:5': "They admire the stonework, and he answers the compliment with a demolition notice.",
    '21:6': "Not one stone left on another. The most permanent thing they could point at is given a deadline.",
    '21:8': "\"Take heed that ye be not deceived.\" The first warning about the end is about being misled, not about suffering.",
    '21:9': "\"The end is not by and by.\" Wars and upheavals are explicitly disqualified as signs of the end.",
    '21:12': "\"But before all these.\" Persecution comes first, and it is the part that concerns them personally.",
    '21:13': "\"It shall turn to you for a testimony.\" The trial becomes the pulpit; there was no other way to that courtroom.",
    '21:14': "They are told not to rehearse a defence. Preparation is forbidden, which is harder than it sounds.",
    '21:15': "\"I will give you a mouth and wisdom.\" The words are supplied, not summoned from within.",
    '21:16': "Betrayed by parents and brethren, and some of them killed. He does not promise that all will survive.",
    '21:17': "\"Hated of all men for my name's sake.\" The hatred is not for their failings but for the name.",
    '21:18': "Not a hair shall perish, said immediately after some are told they will die. Two kinds of safety are in view.",
    '21:19': "\"In your patience possess ye your souls.\" Endurance is how the soul is kept, not how it is earned.",
    '21:20': "Armies around Jerusalem is the concrete sign, and the instruction attached to it is to leave.",
    '21:21': "Flee to the mountains and do not go back in. The godly response to that day is evacuation.",
    '21:23': "Woe to the pregnant and nursing. He counts the cost of the judgment in the bodies least able to run.",
    '21:24': "\"Until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.\" Even the trampling has a term set on it.",
    '21:25': "Signs in sun and moon, and distress with perplexity on earth. The confusion is part of the description.",
    '21:26': "\"Men's hearts failing them for fear\" of what is coming. Anticipation does more damage than the event.",
    '21:27': "The Son of man comes in a cloud. Whatever else is obscure, the arrival is not secret.",
    '21:28': "\"Look up, and lift up your heads.\" The posture commanded in the collapse is the opposite of the crowd's.",
    '21:30': "Leaves on the trees, and everyone knows summer is near. The reading is meant to be that easy.",
    '21:31': "The signs mean the kingdom is near, not that a date can be calculated. Nearness is the whole message.",
    '21:33': "\"Heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away.\" The most solid thing in the room is what he just said.",
    '21:34': "Surfeiting, drunkenness and the cares of this life weigh the heart down alike. Dissipation and worry do the same work.",
    '21:35': "A snare catches what was not watching. The danger is described as ordinary inattention.",
    '21:36': "Watch and pray always. The readiness commanded is prayer, not calculation of dates.",
    '21:37': "Teaching in the temple by day and sleeping on the mount by night. He has no bed in the city that will kill him.",

    # ---- Chapter 22 ----
    '22:2': "They sought how to kill him and feared the people. Fear of the crowd, not of God, set the method.",
    '22:3': "\"Then entered Satan into Judas.\" Luke names the darker cause and still holds Judas responsible for going.",
    '22:4': "He goes to them. The betrayal is not solicited; it is offered.",
    '22:5': "\"They were glad, and covenanted to give him money.\" The bargain is struck with a member of the twelve.",
    '22:6': "He sought opportunity away from the multitude. The crime needs privacy, and so it waits.",
    '22:14': "\"When the hour was come, he sat down.\" Luke's word for the hour has been building for ten chapters.",
    '22:15': "\"With desire I have desired to eat this passover with you before I suffer.\" He wanted this meal.",
    '22:16': "He will not eat it again until it is fulfilled. The supper points forward as much as back.",
    '22:17': "He takes a cup, gives thanks and tells them to divide it. Thanksgiving comes first, on the night of the betrayal.",
    '22:19': "\"This is my body which is given for you.\" Given, in the same room as the man who will sell him.",
    '22:20': "\"The new testament in my blood, which is shed for you.\" A covenant is announced, and the terms are his to keep.",
    '22:21': "\"The hand of him that betrayeth me is with me on the table.\" Judas was served the bread and the cup.",
    '22:22': "Determined, and still a woe. The purpose of God does not make the betrayer an innocent instrument.",
    '22:23': "They ask each other which of them it is. Not one of them is certain it could not be himself.",
    '22:24': "A dispute about who is greatest, minutes after the bread was broken.",
    '22:25': "Gentile rulers dominate and are called benefactors. The title and the practice do not match.",
    '22:26': "\"But ye shall not be so.\" Authority among them is inverted rather than moderated.",
    '22:27': "\"I am among you as he that serveth.\" He settles the argument by pointing at what he is doing at that moment.",
    '22:28': "\"Ye are they which have continued with me in my temptations.\" Said to men about to scatter within hours.",
    '22:29': "The kingdom is appointed to them as the Father appointed it to him. Nothing about it is earned at this table.",
    '22:30': "Eating at his table and sitting on thrones. The reward for following is a meal before it is an office.",
    '22:31': "\"Simon, Simon, Satan hath desired to have you.\" He knows the request was made, and he tells Peter so.",
    '22:32': "\"I have prayed for thee, that thy faith fail not.\" Peter will fall, and the prayer is what he is kept by.",
    '22:33': "\"Lord, I am ready.\" Peter answers the warning with a promise and means every word of it.",
    '22:34': "The denial is dated to that very night. He is told the hour and it changes nothing.",
    '22:35': "\"Lacked ye any thing? And they said, Nothing.\" The record of past provision is put in front of them first.",
    '22:36': "Purse, scrip and sword now. The conditions have changed, and he says so plainly.",
    '22:37': "\"Reckoned among the transgressors.\" He expects to be counted a criminal, and quotes the text that says so.",
    '22:38': "\"It is enough\" — two swords is either absurd or a way of ending a conversation they have misread.",
    '22:39': "He goes to the mount \"as he was wont.\" Judas knew where to look because the habit was fixed.",
    '22:40': "\"Pray that ye enter not into temptation.\" The instruction for that night is the same one he takes himself.",
    '22:41': "A stone's cast away, and kneeling. Luke measures the distance he put between himself and the others.",
    '22:42': "\"Nevertheless not my will, but thine, be done.\" The request is real, and so is the submission.",
    '22:43': "An angel strengthens him. He is not spared the cup, and he is not left unaided.",
    '22:44': "Agony, and sweat like great drops of blood. The physician's gospel records the body under that strain.",
    '22:45': "\"Sleeping for sorrow.\" Luke gives them the kindest possible explanation and still calls it sleep.",
    '22:46': "\"Why sleep ye? rise and pray.\" The remedy offered for exhaustion and grief is prayer, not rest.",
    '22:47': "Judas goes before the crowd and draws near to kiss him. The betrayal is done with a gesture of affection.",
    '22:48': "\"Judas, betrayest thou the Son of man with a kiss?\" He uses the man's name at the moment of the arrest.",
    '22:49': "\"Lord, shall we smite with the sword?\" They ask, and strike before the answer comes.",
    '22:50': "A servant's right ear, cut off by a disciple. The first blood shed that night is from the wrong side.",
    '22:51': "He heals the man who came to arrest him. The last miracle before the cross repairs an enemy.",
    '22:52': "\"Be ye come out, as against a thief?\" He names the theatre of the arrest for what it is.",
    '22:53': "\"This is your hour, and the power of darkness.\" The hour is granted to them; it is not taken.",
    '22:54': "\"Peter followed afar off.\" Close enough to be counted a follower, far enough to deny it.",
    '22:55': "He sits down among them at their fire. The denial begins with a decision about where to sit.",
    '22:56': "A servant girl recognises him by firelight. The first accuser has no authority over him at all.",
    '22:57': "\"Woman, I know him not.\" Three hours earlier he had promised prison and death.",
    '22:58': "\"Man, I am not.\" The second denial is shorter than the first, and easier.",
    '22:59': "His accent gives him away. The thing he cannot hide is where he comes from.",
    '22:60': "The cock crows while he is still speaking. The timing leaves no room to reinterpret what he has done.",
    '22:61': "\"The Lord turned, and looked upon Peter.\" No word is recorded, and none was needed.",
    '22:62': "He went out and wept bitterly. Luke leaves him there, and the restoration waits until after the resurrection.",
    '22:63': "The men holding him mock and beat him. The abuse starts before any court has found anything.",
    '22:64': "Blindfolded, struck, and told to prophesy. They mock the one office he was never going to defend.",
    '22:67': "\"If I tell you, ye will not believe.\" He answers the question by naming what the question is for.",
    '22:69': "\"Hereafter shall the Son of man sit on the right hand of the power of God.\" Said by a prisoner to his judges.",
    '22:70': "\"Ye say that I am.\" He neither denies it nor gives them the phrasing they wanted.",
    '22:71': "\"We ourselves have heard of his own mouth.\" The only witness they end up needing is him.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 23 ----
    '23:1': "The council cannot execute, so the whole body walks him to the governor. The verdict needs Rome's hand.",
    '23:2': "The charge is rewritten for a Roman ear: tax resistance and a rival king. Blasphemy would not have interested Pilate.",
    '23:3': "\"Art thou the King of the Jews? And he answered him and said, Thou sayest it.\" The only word he gives Pilate.",
    '23:4': "\"I find no fault in this man.\" Pilate's first verdict is an acquittal, and he goes on with the trial anyway.",
    '23:5': "They escalate rather than argue. Volume replaces evidence from here to the end.",
    '23:7': "Pilate finds a jurisdiction and uses it. The first instinct of everyone in this chapter is to pass him on.",
    '23:8': "Herod was glad, and hoped to see a miracle. The man who killed John wants a performance.",
    '23:9': "\"He answered him nothing.\" The only person in the gospel to whom Jesus says absolutely nothing is Herod.",
    '23:11': "A gorgeous robe as a joke. The mockery arrives at the truth by accident.",
    '23:12': "Pilate and Herod are made friends that day. Shared cowardice over one prisoner repairs an old feud.",
    '23:14': "\"I, having examined him before you, have found no fault.\" The acquittal is stated a second time, publicly.",
    '23:15': "\"No, nor yet Herod.\" Two authorities have now cleared him, and the sentence still comes.",
    '23:16': "\"I will therefore chastise him, and release him.\" Innocence established, and a flogging offered as a compromise.",
    '23:18': "\"Away with this man, and release unto us Barabbas.\" The crowd is offered a choice and takes the murderer.",
    '23:19': "Barabbas was in prison for sedition and murder — the very crime Jesus was falsely charged with.",
    '23:20': "Pilate speaks again, willing to release him. Wanting the right outcome is not the same as doing it.",
    '23:21': "\"Crucify him, crucify him.\" The crowd that hung on his words days earlier now sets the sentence.",
    '23:22': "\"Why, what evil hath he done?\" The third acquittal, and the question is never answered.",
    '23:23': "\"The voices of them prevailed.\" Luke says plainly what decided the case: not evidence but noise.",
    '23:24': "Pilate gave sentence that it should be as they required. The governor hands his office to the crowd.",
    '23:25': "The guilty man goes free and the innocent one is delivered to their will. The substitution is literal.",
    '23:26': "Simon of Cyrene, in from the country, is seized and made to carry the cross behind Jesus.",
    '23:27': "Women follow him bewailing. Luke names the only people in the city doing anything like grieving.",
    '23:28': "\"Weep not for me, but weep for yourselves.\" On the way to his execution, he redirects the pity.",
    '23:29': "\"Blessed are the barren\" — in the coming days the childless will be counted fortunate. It is not a comfort.",
    '23:30': "Calling on mountains to fall on you is a request to be buried rather than to face what is coming.",
    '23:31': "\"If they do these things in a green tree, what shall be done in the dry?\" The worst is not what they are watching.",
    '23:32': "Two malefactors are led out with him. He is executed as part of a batch.",
    '23:33': "\"There they crucified him.\" Luke gives the central event of history three words and no description.",
    '23:34': "\"Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do.\" The prayer is made while the nails are going in.",
    '23:35': "\"Let him save himself, if he be Christ.\" The taunt assumes saving himself is what a Christ would do.",
    '23:38': "The charge is posted in three languages. The indictment reads as a proclamation.",
    '23:39': "One of the dying men joins in the mockery. Sharing his suffering changed nothing in him.",
    '23:40': "\"Dost not thou fear God, seeing thou art in the same condemnation?\" A criminal preaches the last sermon before the end.",
    '23:41': "\"We indeed justly, but this man hath done nothing amiss.\" He confesses his own guilt and Christ's innocence in one sentence.",
    '23:42': "\"Lord, remember me when thou comest into thy kingdom.\" He asks for nothing but to be remembered.",
    '23:43': "\"To day shalt thou be with me in paradise.\" No baptism, no amendment of life, no time left to earn it.",
    '23:44': "Darkness from the sixth hour to the ninth. The middle of the day goes dark over the middle of the world.",
    '23:45': "The veil is rent in the midst. The barrier comes down while he is still hanging outside the city.",
    '23:46': "\"Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit.\" He dies quoting a psalm, and he gives up the ghost rather than losing it.",
    '23:47': "The centurion glorifies God and calls him righteous. The first confession after the death is a Roman soldier's.",
    '23:48': "The crowd smote their breasts and went home. Something moved in them, and Luke does not call it faith.",
    '23:49': "His acquaintance and the women from Galilee stood afar off. The women are still there at the end.",
    '23:50': "Joseph is a counsellor, good and just — a member of the body that condemned him.",
    '23:51': "He \"had not consented\" and waited for the kingdom of God. A dissenting vote nobody recorded until now.",
    '23:52': "He went to Pilate and begged the body. Claiming a crucified man publicly ends his standing.",
    '23:53': "Linen, and a tomb never used. The burial is careful, and it is done by men with reputations to lose.",
    '23:56': "They prepare spices and then rest the sabbath according to the commandment. Grief keeps the law.",

    # ---- Chapter 24 ----
    '24:1': "They come at first light with spices, expecting a corpse. Their faithfulness is the faithfulness of mourners.",
    '24:3': "They find not the body. The resurrection begins, for them, as an absence and a problem.",
    '24:5': "\"Why seek ye the living among the dead?\" The question treats the resurrection as the obvious thing.",
    '24:6': "\"Remember how he spake unto you.\" The remedy for their bewilderment is the words they already had.",
    '24:7': "The angels quote his own prediction back to them: delivered, crucified, and the third day risen.",
    '24:8': "\"And they remembered his words.\" Faith here is recollection, not a new revelation.",
    '24:10': "Mary Magdalene, Joanna, Mary the mother of James. Luke names the witnesses whose testimony courts would not accept.",
    '24:11': "\"Their words seemed to them as idle tales.\" The apostles' first response to the resurrection is disbelief.",
    '24:12': "Peter runs, sees the linen, and departs wondering. He gets the evidence and not yet the meaning.",
    '24:13': "Two disciples walking away from Jerusalem on the third day, going home because it is over.",
    '24:15': "\"Jesus himself drew near, and went with them.\" He joins the walk in the wrong direction.",
    '24:16': "\"Their eyes were holden.\" The failure to recognise him is done to them, not by them.",
    '24:17': "He asks what they are discussing, and they stand still, looking sad. He draws the grief out by asking.",
    '24:18': "\"Art thou only a stranger in Jerusalem?\" The one man who knows most is treated as the one who knows least.",
    '24:19': "\"A prophet mighty in deed and word.\" Everything they say about him is true and far short.",
    '24:21': "\"We trusted that it had been he which should have redeemed Israel.\" Past tense. The hope is spoken of as dead.",
    '24:24': "\"But him they saw not.\" An empty tomb by itself left them exactly where they were.",
    '24:25': "\"O fools, and slow of heart to believe all that the prophets have spoken.\" The failure is in reading, not in nerve.",
    '24:26': "\"Ought not Christ to have suffered these things, and to enter into his glory?\" The cross was the road in, not a wrong turn.",
    '24:27': "Beginning at Moses, he expounds all the scriptures concerning himself. The Old Testament is read as being about him.",
    '24:28': "\"He made as though he would have gone further.\" He does not force himself on the two who invited nothing.",
    '24:29': "\"Abide with us: for it is toward evening.\" He stays because he is asked to.",
    '24:30': "The guest takes the bread, blesses and breaks it. He acts as host in a house that is not his.",
    '24:31': "Their eyes are opened in the breaking of bread, and he vanishes. He is known and then not seen.",
    '24:32': "\"Did not our heart burn within us?\" They recognise the effect only after they have identified the cause.",
    '24:33': "They rise the same hour and walk seven miles back in the dark. The news will not wait for morning.",
    '24:34': "\"The Lord is risen indeed, and hath appeared to Simon.\" Peter was found before he could return.",
    '24:35': "\"How he was known of them in breaking of bread.\" The report names the table as where the recognition happened.",
    '24:36': "\"Peace be unto you\" is the first thing he says to the men who abandoned him.",
    '24:37': "They were terrified and supposed they had seen a spirit. The resurrection frightens before it comforts.",
    '24:39': "\"Handle me, and see.\" He offers his body as evidence, and invites them to touch the wounds.",
    '24:41': "\"They yet believed not for joy.\" Luke names a disbelief that comes from the news being too good.",
    '24:43': "He ate a piece of broiled fish in front of them. The proof of the resurrection is a meal.",
    '24:44': "Law, prophets and psalms — the whole of the scriptures is said to have been about this all along.",
    '24:45': "\"Then opened he their understanding.\" Even with the risen Christ in the room, the understanding has to be given.",
    '24:46': "\"Thus it behoved Christ to suffer, and to rise.\" The suffering and the rising are one written necessity.",
    '24:47': "Repentance and remission to all nations, \"beginning at Jerusalem\" — the city that killed him is served first.",
    '24:48': "\"Ye are witnesses of these things.\" Their qualification is having seen it, not having understood it first.",
    '24:49': "\"Tarry ye in the city.\" The first order after the commission is to wait for what they cannot supply.",
    '24:50': "He leads them out and lifts his hands. The last thing he does with his hands is bless.",
    '24:51': "\"While he blessed them, he was parted from them.\" The blessing is never finished; he leaves in the middle of it.",
    '24:52': "They returned to Jerusalem with great joy, having just watched him go.",
    '24:53': "The gospel that opened with a priest struck dumb in the temple ends with disciples praising there continually.",
})
